1066 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 357 0 R
/E (Period of analysis spans 2026-2030. Revenue is projected separately for M&C, FSO/FPSO and Port & Marine Services before cross-checking against management's medium-term revenue guidance. M&C revenue is estimated using a project-by-project backlog approach, while FSO/FPSO and Port revenues are driven by asset utilization, charter contracts, and offshore activity levels.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1067 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 357 0 R
/E (Growth drivers: Our forecast is supported by three key drivers: \(i\) continued execution of the current M&C backlog, \(ii\) commissioning of new FSO assets, including Lac Da Vang and Block B, under long-term charter contracts, and \(iii\) the next domestic investment cycle and offshore wind developments under PDP8. We exclude potential contributions from Ca Voi Xanh, nuclear power projects and future offshore wind auctions, which represent upside to our base case.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1068 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 357 0 R
/E (Costs)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1069 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 357 0 R
/E (Gross margin assumptions: We assume a normalized M&C gross margin of 5%, reflecting historical through-cycle profitability. While recent margins have improved, we do not incorporate a structural margin expansion until greater visibility emerges regarding accounting treatment changes and offshore wind execution efficiency. FSO/FPSO margins gradually improve as new assets enter operation, while Port & Base benefit from operating leverage during periods of higher throughput.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
